// mgeHost64 — LUT 2 of 3: MULTIPLE SCATTERING (tasks/forge-atmosphere.md S2a).
//
// Single scattering alone is a sky that is far too dark and far too saturated: it is the light that
// bounced ONCE, and the real atmosphere bounces a lot — which is most of what makes a clear zenith
// the blue it is rather than near-black, and essentially ALL of what makes an overcast sky bright at
// all. Bruneton computed the higher orders offline into a 4D table. Hillaire's contribution, and the
// reason this phase can rebuild every frame, is the observation that the higher orders are very
// nearly ISOTROPIC and can be collapsed into a 2D (altitude, sun angle) term with an ANALYTIC
// infinite-bounce sum:
//
//     F_ms = 1 / (1 - f)          where f is the fraction re-scattered per bounce
//
// That closed form is what replaces the recursion — this LUT integrates ONE bounce and then sums the
// rest algebraically, which is why 32x32 texels and a handful of directions are enough.
//
// ⚠ THE MEDIUM IS NOT DEFINED HERE — atmosphere.h.fsl owns it (see the warning at its top).
//
// ⚠ AND ITS ALTITUDE AXIS IS 32 TEXELS OVER 100 km — 3.2 km PER TEXEL, WHICH IS WIDER THAN THE
// WHOLE DECK. The deck (1.0-2.2 km in the gate's configuration) falls between the LUT's first two
// rows, so an in-cloud lookup here interpolates between a ground-level sample and one above the lid
// — both of them correct, neither of them inside the cloud. Widening the r axis, or making it
// non-linear near the ground, would change the CLEAR sky too and would therefore have to be its own
// step with its own A/B; the parameterisation is Hillaire's and it is tuned for an 8 km scale
// height, not for a 1.2 km slab sitting in the bottom 2% of the range.
//
// ⚠⚠ SO THE CLOUD IS NO LONGER IN THIS LUT'S SOURCE AT ALL (S4a/A2), and its EXTINCTION still is.
// This axis is the reason: a term the axis cannot resolve was the deck's ONLY source of multiple
// scattering, and combined with the `1/(1-f)` sum below — which has no conservation law bounding it
// — the measured result was that E_sky floored at 55% of the incoming flux however thick the lid
// got, and reached 458% of it at tau 3. The deck now gets a delta-Eddington two-stream slab instead
// (Atmosphere::deckSolve, consumed in atmos_skyview), whose defining property is R + T <= 1 by
// construction. What is left in this LUT is the AIR's multiple scattering under a lid, which is
// what this parameterisation was always good for.
//
// ⚠ AND THE SUM DIVERGES IF f REACHES 1. Physically f < 1 always (a bounce cannot return more than
// it received), but a thick, near-non-absorbing aerosol — Foggy at ssa 0.98, Blizzard at 0.95, both
// real rows in the S1 table — pushes it close, and float arithmetic does the rest. The clamp below
// is not a tolerance, it is the guard between "a bright whiteout" and "a NaN that propagates through
// the sky-view LUT into the ambient and out into every lit surface in the frame".
//
// ⚠⚠ S4a WALKED STRAIGHT AT THAT CLAMP, AND A2 IS WHY IT NO LONGER DOES. A cloud deck is
// CONSERVATIVE — single-scattering albedo 0.9999, because a water cloud is bright precisely because
// it does not absorb — so `fms` under a full lid was driven hard toward 1 and the clamp became a
// hard ceiling of F = 50 rather than a guard that never fires. The measurement was decisive: `f` was
// IDENTICAL TO FIVE DECIMALS across a 16x change in the deck's optical depth. That is the sentence
// this file's own warning predicted, and the answer it named — *"a two-stream Eddington source for
// the DECK ONLY is the bounded fallback ... a second model, and therefore its own decision"* — is
// what A2 built, in Atmosphere::deckSolve(). With the cloud's scattering out of the sum below, `f`
// is an air-only quantity again and the clamp goes back to being a guard.
//
// ⚠ THAT MAKES THE GATE'S `MS SERIES` ROW A PREDICTION, WHICH IS THE POINT OF LEAVING IT WIRED: it
// read `**PINNED**` at 0.98 under the old source and must now come back off its ceiling. If it is
// still pinned, A2 did not reach this LUT and nothing downstream of it should be believed.

#include "atmosphere.srt.h"
#include "atmosphere.h.fsl"   // THE MEDIUM — one copy, see the warning at its top

// ⚠ THESE TWO ARE THE PHASE'S REAL COST DIAL, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN AN ASSUMPTION.
// The plan expected the SKY-VIEW march to be the budget; it is not. Measured on the harness scene:
// raising the transmittance LUT from 40 to 128 steps cost +0.01 ms, and HALVING the sky-view march
// from 32 steps to 16 saved 0.01 ms — while the whole four-pass chain sits at 0.37 ms. Nearly all of
// it is here, because this pass is 32x32 texels x 64 directions x 20 steps = 1.3M marched samples,
// each with a transmittance fetch and two ray-sphere tests, against the sky-view LUT's 0.66M.
//
// So the direction count is driven from the cbuffer (marchP.z) rather than being baked in — the
// harness runs minimized and cannot click, and a cost dial nobody can reach unattended is one that
// never gets measured. The step count below is the BASE budget; S4a's segmented schedule adds the
// deck's own steps on top of it whenever a ray actually crosses the lid, so a clear sky still marches
// exactly ATMOS_MS_STEPS times and pays nothing for a deck that is not there.
#define ATMOS_MS_MAX_SQRT_DIRS 8      // 8x8 = 64 directions; the cbuffer picks 2..8
#define ATMOS_MS_STEPS         20

ROOT_SIGNATURE(ComputeRootSignature)
NUM_THREADS(8, 8, 1)
void CS_MAIN( SV_DispatchThreadID(uint3) DispatchThreadID )
{
    INIT_MAIN;

    int2 px = int2(DispatchThreadID.xy);
    int  w  = (int)gAtmosParams.lutDims2.x;
    int  h  = (int)gAtmosParams.lutDims2.y;
    if (px.x >= w || px.y >= h)
    {
        RETURN();
    }

    float2 uv = (float2(px) + f2(0.5f)) / float2(float(w), float(h));
    float r, muSun;
    atmosMultiScatterParams(gAtmosParams, uv, r, muSun);

    float Rg = gAtmosParams.planet.x;
    float Rt = gAtmosParams.planet.y;
    // The sun placed in a canonical frame: only the ANGLE between the sun and a direction matters to
    // an isotropic term, so the azimuth is free and this LUT has no third axis.
    float3 sunDir = float3(sqrt(max(0.0f, 1.0f - muSun * muSun)), 0.0f, muSun);
    float3 origin = float3(0.0f, 0.0f, r);

    // L2 = the second-order in-scatter (what one bounce of the single-scattered field delivers back)
    // fms = the fraction re-scattered per bounce, i.e. the ratio the geometric series runs on.
    float3 L2  = f3(0.0f);
    float3 fms = f3(0.0f);

    // ⚠ THE WEIGHT IS DERIVED FROM THE LIVE COUNT, NOT FROM THE MAXIMUM. A sphere quadrature whose
    // weight and whose sample count disagree does not get noisier — it gets BIASED, by exactly the
    // ratio between them, and a uniformly-too-bright sky is the one error that looks like a
    // calibration question rather than a bug. ([[feedback_skip_optimisation_must_match_kernel]])
    int   nDirs = (int)clamp(gAtmosParams.marchP.z, 2.0f, float(ATMOS_MS_MAX_SQRT_DIRS));
    const float sphereWeight = 4.0f * ATMOS_PI / float(nDirs * nDirs);

    for (int di = 0; di < nDirs; ++di)
    {
        for (int dj = 0; dj < nDirs; ++dj)
        {
            // A uniform sphere lattice: cos(theta) uniform IS uniform in solid angle, so every
            // direction carries the same weight and the integral is a plain sum — the same property
            // scenecal.h's Fibonacci table is chosen for.
            float u0 = (float(di) + 0.5f) / float(nDirs);
            float u1 = (float(dj) + 0.5f) / float(nDirs);
            float cosT = 1.0f - 2.0f * u0;
            float sinT = sqrt(max(0.0f, 1.0f - cosT * cosT));
            float phi  = 2.0f * ATMOS_PI * u1;
            float3 rd  = float3(sinT * cos(phi), sinT * sin(phi), cosT);

            // How far this ray runs: to the ground if it hits, otherwise to the top.
            float tGround = atmosRaySphere(origin, rd, Rg);
            float tTop    = atmosRaySphere(origin, rd, Rt);
            bool  hitG    = (tGround > 0.0f);
            float tMax    = hitG ? tGround : max(tTop, 0.0f);
            if (tMax <= 0.0f) { continue; }

            // ⚠⚠ THE STEP BUDGET IS SEGMENTED AT THE DECK, AND OF THE THREE MARCHES THIS IS THE
            // ONE THAT COULD NOT SEE IT AT ALL. 20 uniform steps over a ray that runs to the top of
            // the atmosphere is ~25 km per step against a 1.2 km lid — the slab falls between two
            // samples and contributes nothing. And it fails in the worst direction: a lid is bright
            // BECAUSE of multiple scattering, so an MS LUT blind to the slab returns a DARK overcast,
            // which is what everyone expects an overcast sky to look like. It would have passed an
            // eyeball test while being the opposite of the physics.
            float4 planT; float3 planN;
            int nSteps = atmosMarchPlan(gAtmosParams, origin, rd, tMax,
                                        ATMOS_MS_STEPS, int(gAtmosParams.marchP.w),
                                        ATMOS_MARCH_UNIFORM, planT, planN);

            float3 throughput = f3(1.0f);
            float3 lSum = f3(0.0f);
            float3 fSum = f3(0.0f);
            // The phase function is ISOTROPIC here on purpose: the whole approximation is that after
            // the first bounce the field has lost its directionality, and using the real HG lobe
            // would be claiming a directionality the 2D table has no axis to store. The cloud's
            // scattering joins the same sum for the same reason — its forward lobe is a statement
            // about the FIRST bounce, and this LUT is everything after it.
            const float isoPhase = ATMOS_INV_4PI;

            for (int s = 0; s < nSteps; ++s)
            {
                float ta, tb;
                atmosMarchStep(planT, planN, s, tMax, ATMOS_MARCH_UNIFORM, ta, tb);
                float dt = tb - ta;
                float t  = ta + dt * 0.5f;
                float3 p = origin + rd * t;
                float  ri = length(p);
                float  hi = ri - Rg;

                float3 scaR, scaM, scaC, extAir, extCloud;
                atmosMediumAt(gAtmosParams, hi, scaR, scaM, scaC, extAir, extCloud);
                // ⚠⚠ THE DECK'S SCATTERING IS NOT IN THIS SUM ANY MORE (S4a/A2), AND ITS EXTINCTION
                // STILL IS. That is the whole of what this LUT got wrong. Read the warning at the top
                // of this file: a conservative lid drove `f` toward 1, the geometric sum `1/(1-f)`
                // has nothing bounding it, and the 3.2 km/texel altitude axis could not resolve a
                // 1.2 km slab anyway — so the term this LUT returned inside the deck was interpolated
                // between a ground sample and one above the lid, and it stopped depending on tau
                // entirely. The deck's own multiple scattering is now a bounded two-stream slab
                // (Atmosphere::deckSolve, read in atmos_skyview), so removing it here is what stops
                // it being counted twice — and it makes `f` an AIR-ONLY quantity again, which is the
                // checkable prediction: the gate's MS SERIES row should come back off its ceiling.
                //
                // ⚠ THE EXTINCTION STAYS because the air under a lid really is in shadow. Dropping
                // it as well would have made this LUT blind to the deck in the other direction.
                float3 sca = scaR + scaM;
                float3 ext = extAir + extCloud;

                float3 stepT = exp(-ext * dt);
                // Sun visibility along this sample's own shadow ray. A ground hit means the sample
                // sits in the planet's own shadow — the term that makes the night side dark and, at
                // twilight, makes the EARTH'S SHADOW a real edge in the sky rather than a fade.
                float  muS = dot(normalize(p), sunDir);
                float3 sunT = f3(0.0f);
                if (atmosRaySphere(p, sunDir, Rg) < 0.0f)
                {
                    float2 tuv = atmosTransmittanceUv(gAtmosParams, ri, muS);
                    // ⚠ THE COVER-WEIGHTED MEAN BEAM. The LUT now carries the air in .rgb and the
                    // deck alone in .a (see atmos_transmittance), and THIS LUT IS ITSELF A MEAN
                    // FIELD — one isotropic term for the whole sky — so the mean transmittance
                    // `(1-c) + c*T_deck` is exactly the quantity it wants. atmos_skyview, which is
                    // not a mean field, takes the two arms separately instead.
                    float4 tr = SampleLvlTex2D(gAtmosTransmittance, gSamplerBilinearClamp, tuv, 0);
                    sunT = tr.rgb * lerp(1.0f, tr.a, saturate(gAtmosParams.deckMix.x));
                }

                // Energy-conserving integration of a constant source over the step (Hillaire): the
                // analytic integral of S*(1-exp(-ext*dt))/ext, which is exact for a step over which
                // the medium is treated as constant and is what keeps a coarse march unbiased
                // instead of merely noisy.
                float3 sInt = (f3(1.0f) - stepT) / max(ext, f3(1.0e-9f));

                lSum += throughput * (sca * isoPhase * sunT) * sInt * gAtmosParams.solar.xyz;
                // fSum accumulates the re-scattered FRACTION: the same integral with the source set
                // to 1 instead of to the sun, i.e. "if this volume were lit by unit radiance from
                // everywhere, how much would it send on".
                //
                // ⚠⚠ THE 4pi THAT USED TO LIVE HERE NOW LIVES AT THE SPHERE SUM, AND MOVING IT IS
                // THE WHOLE FIX. The original catch was real: on the S2b gate's first run `f` came
                // out 4*pi ~= 12.6 times too large, saturated the clamp below, and the geometric
                // series summed to 50 instead of ~1.06 — measured zenith 127656 cd/m2 against a
                // clear-sky band of 2000-8000, E_sky 675 klx against ~14. But the REPAIR put the
                // factor in the step loop, where it happens to give `f` the right number and leaves
                // `L2` — which needs the same 1/4pi as a SPHERE AVERAGE — without one. See the note
                // at `L2 +=` below: the sky then ran 3.3x (red) to 8.2x (blue) over its own first
                // order for the whole of S2-S4a, and the ENERGY row read 112%.
                //
                // ⚠ AND THE SUN WAS PERFECTLY FINE THROUGHOUT (103 klx direct-normal, inside the
                // band), which is exactly why the gate has TWO tables. The beam does not pass
                // through this LUT at all, so every sun-side check passed while the sky was out —
                // and q and sun%, being scale-free, could not see it either. It took the NIT row in
                // absolute cd/m2 to catch the 47x, and the ENERGY row plus a CPU quadrature of the
                // same integral's first order to catch the 4pi hiding behind it.
                fSum += throughput * sca * sInt;

                throughput *= stepT;
            }

            // The ground closes the loop: light that reached the surface comes back up Lambertian and
            // is available to be scattered again. Leaving it out is what makes a naive multiscatter
            // term too dark over bright terrain — and the ground albedo is a real boundary condition
            // of the medium (g_skyAlbedo), not a fudge.
            if (hitG)
            {
                float3 pg   = origin + rd * tMax;
                float3 ng   = normalize(pg);
                float  muSg = dot(ng, sunDir);
                if (muSg > 0.0f)
                {
                    float2 tuv = atmosTransmittanceUv(gAtmosParams, Rg, muSg);
                    float4 gTr = SampleLvlTex2D(gAtmosTransmittance, gSamplerBilinearClamp, tuv, 0);
                    float3 gT  = gTr.rgb * lerp(1.0f, gTr.a, saturate(gAtmosParams.deckMix.x));
                    lSum += throughput * gT * muSg * gAtmosParams.solar.xyz
                          * (gAtmosParams.planet.z / ATMOS_PI);
                }
            }

            // ⚠⚠ BOTH SUMS TAKE THE ISOTROPIC PHASE *HERE*, AND THAT IS WHERE THE SKY'S MISSING 4pi
            // WAS. `sphereWeight * isoPhase` is `1/N` — the plain MEAN over the lattice — and a mean
            // is what both quantities are: `fms` is the mean re-scattered fraction, and `L2` is the
            // MEAN INCIDENT RADIANCE, because the second-order source is
            // `sigma_s * (1/4pi) * integral(L1 dw)` and that 1/4pi is the sphere AVERAGE, not a
            // second phase function.
            //
            // It used to read `fSum += ... * isoPhase` inside the step loop and `* sphereWeight`
            // out here. For `fms` those two cancel to 1/N and the answer was right — which is
            // exactly why the error survived: the note further up records catching `f` at 4pi too
            // large and fixing it by putting isoPhase in the STEP LOOP, and nobody re-derived L2,
            // which needed the same 4pi and never got it. `lSum` already carries isoPhase as its
            // FIRST-BOUNCE PHASE FUNCTION, so multiplying by a bare `sphereWeight` left it
            // 4pi = 12.566x too large. Measured (mieMul 0, so the medium is pure Rayleigh + ozone
            // and every coefficient is Bruneton's):
            //
            //     L_ms(camera)  (40.03, 46.44, 62.94) W/m2/sr   against a FIRST-ORDER field whose
            //     maximum, at the horizon, is (12.0, 12.9, 10.7) and whose zenith is (0.75, 1.39,
            //     2.71). A MEAN over the sphere that is 3.3-5.9x the field's own MAXIMUM is not a
            //     tuning disagreement, it is arithmetic — and /4pi lands it at (3.19, 3.70, 5.01),
            //     which is where a mean of that field belongs.
            //
            // The sky-view march adds `sca * ms` on top of single scattering, so the whole clear sky
            // was carrying it: the zenith read 3.3x (red) to 8.2x (blue) over its own first order and
            // the ENERGY row said 112% of the flux arriving at the top of the atmosphere.
            L2  += lSum * (sphereWeight * isoPhase);
            fms += fSum * (sphereWeight * isoPhase);
        }
    }

    // ⚠ THE GUARD THAT MATTERS. f < 1 physically; the clamp is what stops a thick non-absorbing row
    // (Foggy, Blizzard) from turning the geometric sum into a division by ~0 and pushing an Inf into
    // the sky-view LUT — from where it would reach the SH, the ambient, and every lit surface.
    float3 f = min(fms, f3(0.98f));
    float3 F = f3(1.0f) / max(f3(1.0f) - f, f3(0.02f));

    // ⚠ ALPHA CARRIES THE **UNCLAMPED** f, AND IT IS THERE TO ANSWER ONE QUESTION: IS THE CLAMP A
    // GUARD THAT NEVER FIRES, OR A CEILING THE ANSWER IS SITTING ON? Those two look identical from
    // outside — the LUT returns a finite, plausible number either way — and the difference decides
    // whether an overcast level miss is the CLOUD MODEL or this one line.
    // [[project_forge_exposure_rail_instrument]] is the same lesson: a pinned servo looks converged.
    // Nothing shades with it; atmos_sh.comp reads it at the deck's altitude for the gate. Unclamped
    // on purpose — min(fms, 0.98) read back as 0.98 cannot distinguish 0.981 from 0.9999.
    float fRaw = dot(fms, float3(0.2126f, 0.7152f, 0.0722f));

    Write2D(gAtmosOutA, px, float4(L2 * F, fRaw));

    RETURN();
}
